# 313 -> 308 (previous): §6.4, §6.4.2, §6.4.3, §6.4.4 and §6.5.1 — but the number is the
# smaller half of what changed. conformance/reject/ IS GONE.
#
# It held 27 hand-written programs, and the plan forbids it in as many words. §3: "`expects` is
# a value, an exception class, or a compile-time error." §4: a template "still carries
# sections[] and expects and still goes through the same emitter", and "this applies to the
# corpus, not to a subset chosen at authoring time... I twice proposed restricting stitching to
# the 'genuinely combinatorial' chapters and hand-writing the rest; that is the same
# scope-narrowing as cutting the generator outright". A second authoring path beside the
# generator was exactly that, and it had grown to 27 files.
#
# What was actually wrong with it, beyond the rule: the section claim and the expected
# diagnostic were TYPED into a comment beside each program by whoever wrote it. Nothing tied
# either to a template, so the cardinality gate could not see a rejection at all — a section
# whose rules are half "this is legal" and half "this is an error" could be covered on the
# legal half alone and still count. Both are now derived from sections() and expect(), and
# rejections appear in SECTIONS.tsv beside the running cases.
#
# Three capabilities were needed and none of them is a special case:
#   Val.compileError(diagnostic)  the third kind of expects, with the diagnostic substring that
#                                 stops a rejection counting when the compiler rejected the
#                                 program for an unrelated reason
#   Registry.REJECT               a template that yields no value, so it fills no hole and the
#                                 Stitcher can never stitch one into a running case
#   Units                         a template whose program is SEVERAL compilation units, which
#                                 §6.6.2 needs: §7.3 allows one package declaration per unit, so
#                                 a single-file version of that program does not exist
#
