# REJECTED (2b: a constant int representable in T gives T), and `c ? aString : aStringBuffer`
# was ACCEPTED though the last bullet calls it a compile-time error -- Java 1.0 has no
# least-upper-bound rule, so "a common supertype" was invented, not read.
#
# All five bullets are now transcribed in the spec's order, which is load-bearing: byte/short
# is tested before the constant rule and both before promotion. Coverage is one snippet per
# bullet (conformance/gen/Lib15.java) rather than one program touching several, because a
# single `// JLS 15.24` marker is emitted identically by a case that tests one path and by one
# that tests all of them -- which is exactly how this stayed hidden under five earlier cases.
