# when the type is char and "65" when it is int. The error half is
# reject/ch15_conditional_incompatible_refs.java, paired with t15.cond.ref.widen so that a
# compiler rejecting every mixed-reference conditional fails the positive case.
#
# §6.1, §6.2, §6.3 and §6.3.1 by conformance/gen/Lib6.java, plus reject cases for the two
# directions §6.3 distinguishes -- a field initializer reading a field declared later is an
# error, the same read from a CONSTRUCTOR is not, and the spec states the rule as exactly that
# exception ("needs to appear before it is used only when the use is in a field initialization
# expression"). Rejecting both would be as wrong as accepting both, so both are pinned.
# §6.3.1 reproduces its worked output verbatim, "x=0, T6Hide.x=1", because that text IS the
# claim.
#
# §6.6.2 is the divergence. javelinac implemented protected access as "same package or
# subclass" -- §6.6.1 -- and stopped there, so it accepted §6.6.7's worked compile-time error.
# §6.6.2 constrains HOW a subclass in another package may reach the member: "If the access is
# by a qualified name Q.Id, where Q is an ExpressionName, then the access is permitted if and
# only if the type of the expression Q is S or a subclass of S", where S is the subclass in
# whose body the use occurs -- NOT the declaring class. So inside Point3d, `p.x` on a
# Point-typed parameter is an error while `q.x` on a Point3d-typed one is fine, because
# Point3d "is not involved in the implementation of a Point".
